<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<catalogue id="a7b5-e06b-5105-0223" name="Hapsburgian Burgundy and Flanders" revision="4" battleScribeVersion="2.03" authorName="Jono Firth" authorUrl="https://www.greatescapegames.co.uk/uploads/images/wysiwyg/home-shortcuts/coe/medieval/Habsburgian_Burgundy_and_Flanders_Aug_15th_2012.pdf" library="false" gameSystemId="c749-87c2-0d4a-5e29" gameSystemRevision="12" xmlns="http://www.battlescribe.net/schema/catalogueSchema">
  <readme>After Charles the Bold’s death at the Battle of Nancy on the 5th of January 1477, the King of France Louis XI tried to conquer the Burgundian states. Mary of Burgundy, Charles the Bold’s heiress, married Maximilian von Habsburg, Archduke of Austria, on the 21st of April 1477. The new Duke of Burgundy attempted to reorganize the army of his father in law: he restored the compagnies d’ordonnances but, because of the lack of money, could not afford a large permanent army like his predecessor, relying instead on mercenaries. This army fought against the French Ordonnance army at the Battle of Guinegatte (7th August 1479) and in several sieges and skirmishes until peace was signed in December 1482.

A Habsburgian Burgundy army must include a general and at least two units of Flemish Pikemen.</readme>
